===============================================================================
Protocol definitions
===============================================================================

The Protocol Buffers definitions under `app/src/main/proto/` are taken from
Tesla's open-source repository:

  teslamotors/vehicle-command — https://github.com/teslamotors/vehicle-command
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

These `.proto` files are used as the machine-readable protocol contract for the
Vehicle Security (VCSEC) and Infotainment domains. The build generates lite Java
bindings from them (e.g. `*OuterClass`). Any modification to those files is limited
to build compatibility; the protocol semantics are the upstream definitions.

The protocol implementation in this project (`app/src/main/java/com/teslalink/app/protocol/`,
`ble/`, `vehicle/`) is an independent Kotlin implementation written for T-Dash against
those published definitions and the official Go reference in the same upstream repository.

===============================================================================
Third-party reference material (not shipped, not copied)
===============================================================================

During development, community implementations of Tesla's Bluetooth key protocol — in
particular `esphome/tesla-ble` and its forks, licensed under AGPL-3.0 — were used only as
an observational reference for protocol behaviour on real vehicles. No source code,
message framing, or structure was copied from any AGPL-licensed project into this
repository, and no AGPL-licensed code is distributed with this product. Reference clones
are kept outside the repository and are excluded by `.gitignore`.

===============================================================================
Optional third-party network services
===============================================================================

This app talks to a vehicle over Bluetooth Low Energy only. Network access is used for
three optional features, and every such request is sent directly from the user's device to
the third party — never through a server operated by this project:

  * Map tiles for the Stats page: AMap public raster tiles
    (https://wprd01.is.autonavi.com/appmaptile). This endpoint is not part of the AMap
    Open Platform API; it is intended for personal, non-commercial use only. Users who
    need commercial or high-volume usage must switch to a licensed tile source.
  * Reverse geocoding for the accident assistant: AMap Web Service API with the user's own
    API key, falling back to OpenStreetMap Nominatim.
  * Weather for the dashboard: AMap Web Service API with the user's own API key
    (disabled by default).

OpenStreetMap / Nominatim usage is subject to the OpenStreetMap Foundation usage policy.
AMap usage is subject to AutoNavi/AMap terms; an API key must be applied for by the user.

